        618
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "The city of Athens",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 40,
      "bbox": [
        438,
        194,
        655,
        876
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Even at the end of the fourth century, the size of the city was extremely small\nby modern standards, one area being within easy walking distance of another.\nAlthough large and expensively equipped private houses were not unknown in\nAthens, most were still basically simple, consisting of a series of small rooms\narranged round an inner court. By contrast, private and public money had for\ngenerations been spent on public buildings, whether for heated political discus-\nsion, athletic or theatrical competitions, legal wrangling or religious celebra-\ntions. It was there that the real life of the polis had always been lived, and in the\nfourth century Athenian politicians, in their efforts to castigate their opponents’\nindulgence in private comfort and display, nostalgically simplified the more\npublic-spirited attitudes of the fifth-century leaders, as in the following speech\nascribed to Demosthenes:",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 1,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 41,
      "bbox": [
        658,
        194,
        876,
        876
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "‘The buildings which they left to adorn our city—the temples and harbours\nand all that goes with them—are on a scale which their successors cannot hope\nto surpass; look at the Propylaia, the docks, the colonnades and all the other\nadornments of the city which they have bequeathed to us. And the private houses\nof those in power were so modest and in keeping with the title of our constitu-\ntion that, as those of you who have seen them know, the houses of Themistokles,\nKimon and Aristides, the famous men of those days, were no grander than those\nof their neighbours. But today, my friends, … some of the private individuals\nwho hold any public office have built private houses which are grander not only\nthan those of the ordinary run of citizens but even than our public buildings,\nand others have bought and cultivate estates on a scale undreamed of before.’\n([Demosthenes], On Organisation 13.28–9) (World of Athens, 2.38)",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 2,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    }
